  "teaching": {
    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M7 V05: Diffraction and the wave model. Why does light spread after passing an edge or narrow opening? (design)",
    "causalExplanation": "You can see the same thing with water. In a ripple tank, straight waves roll towards a barrier with a gap in it. Beyond a wide gap, the waves carry on mostly straight, bending only a little at the edges. Make the gap about one wavelength wide, and the waves spread out in semicircles, right round into the regions behind the barrier. This spreading is called diffraction. And light does it too, because light is a wave. Why do waves spread like that? In the 1600s, Christiaan Huygens had an idea. Treat every point on a wavefront as a tiny source, sending out its own little circular wave, called a wavelet. A moment later, the new wavefront is the line touching the front of every wavelet. On a wide, open wavefront, each wavelet has neighbours on both sides, so the sideways parts fill in, and the front just moves straight ahead. Now put a barrier in the way, with a gap. Only the points in the gap can send wavelets on. In the middle of the gap, the wavelets still have neighbours, so that part of the front stays straight. But at each edge, there's nothing beyond to fill in. Those wavelets spread round, into the shadow behind the barrier. A single edge does the same thing. Make the gap narrower, and more of the front is near an edge. In a very narrow gap, the whole front behaves like one wavelet, and it spreads out like a semicircle.",
