    "visualSystem": "# Year 12 Physics M7 V05: Diffraction and the wave model. Why does light spread after passing an edge or narrow opening? (design)\n\nStatus: Revision 3 (13 Nov 2026): visual-standard top-up (stage list, question-card pictures, givens strips, five-line notes, simulation engines named, root rules R1 to R4), visuals only. Narration unchanged. Needs a Codex error check before plates and voicing. Previously Revision 2 (7 Nov 2026): brief 191 findings resolved and visual upgrade to maths-visual-standard.md.\n\nNarration 6,289 characters, 16 scenes, about 542 s, counted by script with the build parser rules (total = sum of scene strings; see \"Narration length estimate\"). Opens on v04's final frame.\n\nScope: fifth video of Year 12 Physics Module 7 (The Nature of Light, Physics Stage 6, 2017 syllabus), first video of the inquiry question \"What evidence supports the classical wave model of light and what predictions can be made using this model?\". Templates as `phys-y12-m7-v01.md`. No pre-design pack exists; the pack work is done here. Site lessons read in full (6 Nov, read-only, through the brief 168 text extracts, hashes checked): lesson 4 (orient and predict, single-slit diffraction with Figure 1, diffraction gratings, the grating worked example, comparing patterns with Figure 4, revisit, \"Analyse 6 marks\", \"Assess 6 marks\", SAQ 1 and 2, retrieve with Fraunhofer's grating), `C:/Users/kygs/hscscience/subjects/physics/year12/module7/lesson04.sa.html`, SHA256 `c0e0238c1a22bff8c12e572f14a8b6c95599a291e82d17603642dd4e7b32fd1d`; lesson 10 (see `phys-y12-m7-v02.md`; sections `wave-model-evidence` and `wave-vs-particle-history` used here, with \"Analyse 7 marks\" on Newton and Huygens), `.../module7/lesson10.sa.html`, SHA256 `3c6b866e16c1a9a0da5fd4b379c32c64cd1abbb96412642f19fdcb41efca4bc1` (both recomputed 6 Nov; match the route). Route entry `phys-y12-m7-v05`, sections `single-slit-diffraction`, `compare-diffraction-patterns` (lesson 4), `wave-model-evidence`, `wave-vs-particle-history` (lesson 10). Route start: \"Start from the physical case in the learner question; recall only the earlier result needed for it.\" Route stop: \"Use Huygens wavefronts to explain qualitative diffraction and compare Newton particle evidence. Stop before quantitative interference.\" Next: \"phys-y12-m7-v06: Why do two slits produce regularly spaced bright and dark bands?\" Practical in the route: \"Qualitative diffraction investigation with approved light source\".\n\nOwner rules applied: as v01 (no people, hands, faces or body outlines; Newton, Huygens, Young, Fresnel, Poisson, Arago and Foucault named in words only, never drawn); painted plates for the ripple tank, laser, slide holder, screen and bench; wavefronts, wavelets, barriers, the shadow region, patterns and the Poisson-spot diagram coded and labelled schematic; a 3 s prediction; \"Pause here to answer.\" with a 10 s countdown; brief feedback; original attempts; optional key notes; Australian spelling; no em dashes. Recalled, not re-taught: Year 11 wavefronts, reflection and refraction, wavelength; v01 (light is an EM wave); v02 (Foucault's apparatus, forward reference now paid off).\n\nSyllabus content (Physics Stage 6 (2017), Module 7 The Nature of Light; ledger ids `P7-IQ2-01`, `P7-IQ2-03`), quoted from the ledger:\n\n- `P7-IQ2-01`: \"conduct investigations to analyse qualitatively the diffraction of light (ACSPH048, ACSPH076)\"\n- `P7-IQ2-03`: \"analyse the experimental evidence that supported the models of light that were proposed by Newton and Huygens (ACSPH050, ACSPH118, ACSPH123)\"\n\nSource: https://www.nsw.gov.au/sites/default/files/noindex/2025-03/physics-stage-6-syllabus-2017.docx (page 59)\n\n**Boundary:** starts on v04's final frame (spectroscope slit, light spreading into the shadow, \"?\"). Teaches: diffraction seen with water waves (ripple tank: narrow gap, wide spread); Huygens' principle (every point on a wavefront is a source of wavelets; the new wavefront touches them all); why a gap or edge makes waves spread (the edge wavelets have no neighbours beyond the edge); why spreading is noticeable only when the opening is comparable to the wavelength (light about 500 nm; doorway 2 million wavelengths; sound about 1 m); the single-slit pattern qualitatively (wide central band, fainter side bands, dark gaps from cancellation; narrower slit or longer wavelength, wider pattern); the shadow of an edge; Newton's corpuscles versus Huygens' waves and their predictions; the deciding evidence (diffraction, Foucault 1850, the Poisson spot); the qualitative investigation and laser safety. Stops before a sin θ = mλ and any quantitative interference (v06, v07), polarisation (v08), photons.\n\n## Decisions\n\n- **Learner question (route wording kept).** \"Why does light spread after passing an edge or narrow opening?\" The answer: light is a wave; every point on a wavefront sends out wavelets (3); at an edge or gap, the wavelets at the boundary have no neighbours to keep the front straight, so they spread into the shadow (4); the effect is large only when the opening is comparable to the wavelength (5).\n- **Hook (site lesson 4 prediction box).** The prediction asks: \"Make the slit narrower. Does the central band get narrower or wider?\" (chips \"narrower\", \"wider\"); answer \"wider\" (site misconception).\n- **Water first (Year 11 bridge).** The ripple-tank plate gives a familiar visible case; then the same geometry with light.\n- **Huygens' construction (site lesson 10 Figure 1).** Shown on an open wavefront first (stays straight), then at a gap and at an edge. The phrase \"the line touching the front of every wavelet\" is used rather than \"envelope\".\n- **Scale argument (cause of \"why we rarely see it\").** Doorway about 1 m / 500 nm = 2 × 10⁶ wavelengths; sound at about 340 Hz has λ about 1 m (Year 11 v = 340 m s⁻¹; the HSC data sheet speed of sound in air is 340 m s⁻¹). A 0.1 mm slit is 200 wavelengths of 500 nm light and gives a visibly spread pattern.\n- **Single-slit pattern, qualitative only (route stop).** Narrower slit or longer wavelength gives a wider pattern; the dark gaps come from light from different parts of the slit arriving out of step and cancelling. No a sin θ = nλ (site formula) and no numbers on the pattern; the formula is out of the route's scope for this video.\n- **Newton and Huygens (site \"Analyse 7 marks\" structure).** Revision 2 (brief 191 optional): the comparison is with the simple straight-line particle picture; scene 7 says \"In the simple particle picture\", and author note (c) records that Newton's Opticks discussed inflexion near edges and forces on rays. Competing predictions first (Newton: straight-line corpuscles attracted into denser media, so faster in water and sharp shadows; Huygens: waves, slower in water, spreading), then the discriminating evidence: diffraction into shadows, Foucault's 1850 water result (v02's forward reference), the Poisson spot (Fresnel's wave theory predicted it, Poisson thought it absurd, Arago observed it). Reflection is named as not distinguishing (site trap). Young's interference is named as the next video's evidence. The reason Newton's model held for a century (tiny wavelength makes diffraction hard to notice; Newton's authority) is one sentence.\n- **Investigation (route practical; \"approved light source\").** A class 2 laser (or a filament lamp behind a single slit), an adjustable slit and a screen; observe the central band widen as the slit narrows; compare red and green lasers; laser safety: never look along the beam or at its reflections; remove reflective jewellery is not mentioned (no people). The video shows the method; it does not complete the practical.\n- **Memory aid (scene 10, new):** \"Narrow gap, wide spread.\" See \"Memory aid check\".\n- **Attempts (original).** A1: Huygens explanation of why a narrower slit spreads light more (3 marks). A2: how Foucault's water measurement and diffraction distinguish Newton's and Huygens' models (3 marks). Crossed: A1 \"less light gets through, so the pattern is narrower\"; A2 \"reflection supports the wave model over the particle model\".\n- **Handoff (route next).** Two narrow slits: the spreading waves overlap, and the screen shows regular bands. Next: \"Why do two slits produce regularly spaced bright and dark bands?\". See \"Final frame\".\n- **Colour key:** wavefronts teal lines; Huygens wavelets thin teal circles; barriers dark grey; the geometric shadow pale grey hatching; laser light red (or green where stated); particle predictions grey dots and straight arrows; wave predictions teal.\n- **Colour key (Revision 2, fixed for every scene).** As above. Teal wavefronts and wavelets are a role colour for \"wave model\", never a light colour; wherever the light itself appears (beam, screen pattern, slit view) it is drawn in its true colour: the red laser (650 nm) red, the green laser (532 nm) green. Sound fronts grey. Framed answers in a dark-ink box; crossed chips and their hand tags red; ticks green. Hand-font tags take the colour of the quantity they mark.\n- **Type sizes (Revision 2, 1080p).** Current statement or working line at least 44 px (scene 5 working and the feedback rows 44 to 52 px; framed results 52 to 60 px); live readouts (slit sweep in scenes 1 and 9: slit width and central band width) about 52 px; the duel table's current row 44 px, earlier rows shrunk to a 30 px summary strip; supporting lines and chips 30 to 36 px; hand-font tags at least 30 px; question text 36 px; nothing a student must read below 26 px. All text above the bottom 12% of the frame.\n- **Anchor (Revision 2).** Scene 1's bench uses the delivered laser module and slide holder (brief 191, inspected: a laser body on a post in a bench carrier; a square frame with a clear window on the same carrier) with the reused optical bench and screen carrier, all at one uniform scale (`assets/brief191-qa/bench-fit.json`). That record shows the laser aperture sits lower than the slide window and screen: the build lifts the whole laser plate (uniform scale, never stretched) onto a short coded bench block in the bench's grey so the beam runs level. The ripple tank plate opens scene 2.\n- **Simulation (Revision 2).** The core live control is the slit sweep (scenes 1 and 9): the coded slit narrows 0.20, 0.10, 0.05 mm while the screen pattern widens, with live readouts \"slit width\" and \"central band width\" (red, 2.0 m screen: 13, 26, 52 mm; green: 11, 21, 43 mm; Python, author note g). The readouts show cause and effect; no formula is shown (route stop). Scene 4's gap-narrowing Huygens stage is the second live element.\n- **Stage changes (Revision 2).** Revision 1 held the duel table for scenes 7 and 8 (about 97 s). Now scene 7 builds the table one row at a time at large size, and scene 8 cuts to the evidence: the Foucault row with v02's mirror icon, then a camera change to the Poisson stage (full width) on \"disc\", then back to the completed table on \"won\". No set-up runs past about 70 s (the Huygens stage, scenes 3 and 4).\n- **Question cards (Revision 2).** Each card arrives with its full text and a picture in one move. On the answer side the card shrinks to the top, a givens strip lists the conditions, the picture shows the answer, the key answer line is framed and one classic slip is crossed.\n- **Site sources used:** lesson 4 `single-slit-diffraction` (laser at a slit, central band widening, narrower slit gives wider pattern), `compare-diffraction-patterns` (single-slit intensity shape only), the prediction box and revisit; lesson 10 `wave-model-evidence` (Huygens' principle and Figure 1, the table's \"particles travel in straight lines\"), `wave-vs-particle-history` (Newton, Huygens, Young, Foucault 1850), \"Analyse 7 marks\" (competing predictions, Foucault, dark fringes, Poisson spot, the limit), Q1 (c). Not used: a sin θ = nλ and its numbers, gratings, resolving power (v07), thin films, the photoelectric material.\n- **Site notes (quoted from the site lessons, read 6 Nov; for Codex to log in `site-corrections-phys-y12.md`):**\n  1. Lesson 4, SAQ 2 model answer (b)(ii): \"The central maximum is dimmer, half the slit width means half the area, so less light is transmitted through the slit and the intensity decreases.\" The conclusion is right but the reason is incomplete: half the light passes and it spreads over twice the width, so the peak intensity falls to about a quarter. Proposed fix: \"Half as much light passes, and it is spread over a central maximum twice as wide, so the central intensity falls to about one quarter.\"\n  2. Lesson 10, `wave-model-evidence` table, Polarisation row: \"Longitudinal particles cannot be polarised\". \"Longitudinal\" describes waves, not particles. Proposed fix: \"Longitudinal waves cannot be polarised, and a simple particle stream has no oscillation direction to select.\"\n  3. Lesson 4, `orient-and-predict` warm-up: \"diffraction is most pronounced when the aperture width is… Comparable to or smaller than the wavelength\" alongside the distractor \"Much smaller than the wavelength, light cannot pass through\". Light does pass through apertures much smaller than its wavelength (with very low transmitted power and maximal spreading). Proposed fix: reword the distractor to \"Much larger than the wavelength, so light passes straight through\".\n  Checked and correct: Huygens' principle wording, the narrower-slit result, Foucault 1850 against Newton, the Poisson-spot account, Q1 (c).\n- **Cause chain taught, in order:** (1) water waves spread through a gap (2); (2) every point on a wavefront sends out wavelets; a wide front stays straight because neighbours fill in (3); (3) at a gap or edge the boundary wavelets have no neighbours on the shadow side, so the front curves into the shadow (4); (4) the narrower the gap compared with λ, the more of the front is \"edge\", so the more it spreads (4, 5); (5) light's tiny λ explains why everyday shadows look sharp (5); (6) parts of the slit's light cancel at certain angles, giving dark gaps (6); (7) Newton's model predicts no spreading and faster light in water; observations matched Huygens (7, 8).\n- **Entry knowledge (route):** v04; Year 11 wavefronts, wavelength, reflection, refraction, superposition.\n- **Learner-visible vocabulary:** diffraction (waves spreading as they pass an edge or through a gap, 2); wavefront (a line joining points of a wave that are at the same stage, such as all the crests, 3); wavelet (a small circular wave from one point of a wavefront, 3); geometric shadow (where a straight-line path would leave darkness, 4); corpuscle (Newton's name for a tiny particle of light, 7).\n- **Legacy logic ported:** none. Kit family `m7-diffraction` (sinc envelope and array maxima) is the candidate support for scene 6's single-slit intensity curve only; its limit (each pattern separately normalised) is respected. The Huygens construction is new motion.\n- **New explainer motion:** **Huygens stage** (straight wavefront as a row of points, each emitting a growing teal wavelet; the touching line redrawn each step; barrier with an adjustable gap; a single-edge mode); **ripple view** (top view of the ripple tank with a coded gap barrier and spreading wavefronts); **slit pattern** (coded screen image and intensity curve for two slit widths and two colours, labelled schematic); **model duel** (two columns, Newton and Huygens, predictions and observation rows filling with ticks and crosses); **Poisson diagram** (coded round obstacle, its shadow and a central bright dot, schematic); `Countdown`; `QuestionCard`; `Chip` with `cross`; `KeyNotes`; `Heading`.\n- **Board layout:** heading top-left; the stage centre-left; the duel table or chips on the right; everything above the bottom 12%.\n- **Author notes (not learner copy):**\n  (a) Huygens (Traité de la Lumière, written 1678, published 1690); Newton (Opticks, 1704); Young's interference about 1801 to 1803; Fresnel's prize memoir 1818 to 1819; Poisson's objection and Arago's observation 1818 to 1819; Foucault's water result 1850. Dates appear only as \"1600s\" and \"1850\" in narration.\n  (b) Huygens' original construction does not explain why there is no backward wave or why the spreading is limited; the Fresnel and Kirchhoff refinement adds interference between wavelets. The narration's \"neighbours fill in\" is a fair qualitative statement of the forward envelope.\n  (c) Newton did know of diffraction (\"inflexion\", after Grimaldi) and in Opticks Book III proposed forces acting on rays near edges; the narration's \"In the simple particle picture, straight-line particles should cast sharp shadows\" refers to the simple straight-line corpuscle model, as the syllabus and site frame it (Revision 2 wording, brief 191 optional finding).\n  (d) Sound: v = 340 m s⁻¹ (HSC data sheet value), f = 340 Hz gives λ = 1.0 m.\n  (e) Laser safety: school lasers are class 2 or lower; the narration gives the beam and reflection rule only.\n  (f) Pronunciations: \"Huygens\" (HOY-gens), \"Poisson\" (pwah-SON), \"Arago\" (AH-ra-go), \"Fresnel\" (fray-NEL), \"corpuscles\".\n  (g) Slit-sweep readouts (Revision 2, not narrated, no formula on screen): central band width ≈ 2Lλ/a for a single slit at small angles, L = 2.0 m. Red 650 nm: a = 0.20, 0.10, 0.05 mm give 13.0, 26.0, 52.0 mm. Green 532 nm: 10.64, 21.28, 42.56 mm, shown as 11, 21, 43 mm. Readouts are simulated values from that relation, labelled \"simulated\".\n- **Open decisions for root:**\n  - **1. Plates.** Revision 2: `y12p-slide-holder-v1.png` and `y12p-laser-module-v1.png` were delivered in brief 191 and inspected (clear window and coded-filter safe centre in `assets/brief191-manifest.json`). Reused: `phys-y11-ripple-tank-v1.png`, `phys-y11-optical-bench-v1.png`, `phys-y11-screen-carrier-v1.png`. The slit, the obstacle and all patterns are coded overlays on the holder and screen. Bench height: see \"Anchor (Revision